{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "TinySQL: A Decoder-Only Language Model for Text-to-SQL",
      "body_html": "TinySQL is a decoder-only language model for text-to-SQL. Every part of the model is written from scratch: the byte-level BPE tokenizer, the transformer (grouped-query attention, rotary embeddings, SwiGLU, RMSNorm), the optimizer, the training loop, the data pipeline, a grammar-constrained decoder and the evaluation harness. It is pretrained for about 24 hours on a single NVIDIA A100 GPU on 10 million tokens of educational web text and SQL code, then fine-tuned on WikiSQL."
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "headline": "Controlled Fine-Tuning and Evaluation",
      "body_html": "We fine-tuned four public models of similar size with the same recipe: an identical learning rate sweep, three random seeds, and no pretrained architecture. We report confidence intervals and paired significance tests throughout. On the WikiSQL test set, TinySQL reaches 74.5% execution accuracy with greedy decoding and 76.3% with schema- and value-constrained decoding."
    },
    {
      "index": 2,
      "headline": "Comparison to Other Models",
      "body_html": "TinySQL is about one point below mpt-7b and 27 points above falcon-7b, which were pretrained on 1.3 billion and 1.4 billion tokens respectively. Falcon-7b is pretrained on 1.4 billion tokens, which is about 400 million more than TinySQL. Falcon-7b is 35 points ahead of TinySQL. Pretraining accounts for 24 points of accuracy. Constrained decoding never turned one of TinySQL's correct answers into a wrong one in any paired comparison we ran."
    },
    {
      "index": 3,
      "headline": "Ablation and Label Quality",
      "body_html": "Small-scale ablations show that rotary embeddings and tied embeddings matter far more than grouped-query attention or an SQL-heavy pretraining mix. A hand audit suggests that most remaining aggregation errors are label errors in WikiSQL. All code, results and the tests that verify the components are public."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Why Small Language Models Matter for Text-to-SQL",
      "body_html": "Small language models are appealing for text-to-SQL because they run on a CPU, can sit close to a database, and cost little to fine-tune. Most small models today were pretrained by large teams on trillions of tokens. This report looks at a specific question about them."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "The System Is Built From Scratch",
      "body_html": "The system has no pretrained weights, tokenizers or modeling libraries. Every baseline gets the same fine-tuning recipe and its own learning-rate sweep. Main results are averaged over seeds. The test set is used once, and comparisons are paired and tested for significance."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "A Full Text-to-SQL System",
      "body_html": "The system includes a tokenizer, a model similar to Llama, an optimizer, a data pipeline with deduplication and benchmark cleaning, a grammar-constrained decoder, and evaluation code. All parts are built from scratch and tested together."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Controlled Comparison on WikiSQL",
      "body_html": "The study compared five models on WikiSQL, including four public ones of similar size. It used learning-rate sweeps, three random seeds, a no-pretraining control group, bootstrap confidence intervals, and McNemar tests. The results appear in Section 2."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "A Web+SQL Model Beats Two Benchmarks",
      "body_html": "A model trained on both deliberate web and SQL data came close to the top benchmark and beat the second one. This model also did better than the third benchmark when we limited how much it could say. The weaker models got a bigger lift from this constraint."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "WikiSQL Data Format",
      "body_html": "WikiSQL pairs crowd-written questions with SQL queries over single Wikipedia tables. The queries select one column, optionally aggregate it, and filter with up to four conjunctive conditions. Large pretrained encoder-based systems achieve test execution accuracy in the high nineties, and their authors attribute much of the remaining errors to annotation issues. Recent work cleans the dataset for the LLM era. Multi-table benchmarks like Spider are harder and are not covered here."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "SQL Generation Techniques",
      "body_html": "Researchers have long used two methods to ensure generated SQL queries are correct. One checks partial queries against a parser, as PICARD does. The other runs candidate queries to see if they work. We use a simple byte-level grammar made for WikiSQL queries. A strict mode lets only values from the question through."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Model Comparison",
      "body_html": "Three open models sit in the LLaMA family: Pythia, a smaller model with about 1.3 billion parameters and 1.5 trillion training tokens; a larger model with about 6.9 billion parameters and 10 trillion tokens; and a third model whose exact size and token count remain unpublished. Our architecture mirrors LLaMA's design: RoPE positional encoding, RMSNorm layer normalization, SwiGLU feedforward layers, grouped-query attention heads, and shared word embeddings."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "The Model Code",
      "body_html": "The model code is written by hand on top of PyTorch tensors and autograd. It includes linear and embedding layers, RMSNorm, rotary embeddings, grouped-query attention with a KV cache, SwiGLU, a memory-efficient cross-entropy, sampling, AdamW, and the training loop. No pretrained weights, pretrained tokenizers, or modeling classes from Hugging Face are used. Hugging Face code appears only as a test oracle, to download datasets, and to run the baselines. PyTorch's fused attention kernel is tested against our own implementation."
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "headline": "Hugging Face's Role",
      "body_html": "Hugging Face code plays three roles: it serves as a test oracle, downloading datasets, and running the baselines. Our weights loaded into a reference Llama implementation produce the same logits to within [[2]], for the full [[3]] model. The repository contains [[4]] tests, including exact-resume of training, optimizer agreement with [[5]] to [[6]], and a positive control for decontamination."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Tokenizer Details",
      "body_html": "The tokenizer is a byte-level BPE model with [[2]] tokens, which took up [[4]] merges and [[5]] special tokens. It was trained on [[9]][[10]]MB of text weighted toward SQL, plus WikiSQL questions and column names. Common SQL keywords became single tokens. On FineWeb-Edu, held-out compression was [[15]] bytes per token. On the median SQL file, it was [[16]] bytes per token. Web, code and forum text gets encoded with special tokens disabled."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Model Architecture and Training Details",
      "body_html": "Table~[[1]] and Figure~[[2]] show the model architecture. Weights start from [[3]], with residual output projections scaled by [[4]]. The loss is a custom autograd function that keeps only the logits and one log-sum-exp per token. This lets us fit micro-batches of up to [[5]][[6]][[7]] tokens through a [[8]]-output layer on a [[9]][[10]]GB GPU."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Data Sources and Preprocessing",
      "body_html": "Table~[[1]] lists the sources. The pipeline applies per-source quality filters, such as truncating heavy SQL dumps to keep the schema and drop bulk data rows. It also performs exact deduplication across all sources and uses MinHash-LSH near-duplicate removal for SQL files. From the SQL files, the ones with the most query features—such as SELECT, JOIN, WHERE, aggregates—are kept."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Scanning Pretraining Documents for WikiSQL Questions",
      "body_html": "Every pretraining document was checked for WikiSQL dev and test questions. A shared phrase of two or more words counted as a match. The whole question counted if it was three to four words long. No document matched in the 500 scanned. To show the scanner worked, we planted 12 dev/test questions into 100 real documents. All 12 were found with no false alarms. Questions shorter than two words were too vague to scan, and gold SQL strings were not checked."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Training Setup",
      "body_html": "We train with AdamW~[[1]], weight decay on weight matrices only, gradient clipping at [[4]], autocast and [[5]]. The peak learning rate of [[7]] comes from a sweep on a [[8]] proxy (optimum near [[9]]--[[10]], lowered for the larger model and longer run), with [[11]] warmup steps and cosine decay to [[12]][[13]]."
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "headline": "Training Details",
      "body_html": "Each step is [[14]] tokens; the run is [[15]] steps, i.e.\ [[16]] tokens or about [[17]] FLOPs, roughly [[18]][[19]] the compute-optimal token count for this size~[[20]]. Data is mixed in two phases (Figure~[[21]]): [[22]][[23]] (web/SQL/Stack Exchange/Gretel) for the first [[24]][[25]] of steps, then [[26]][[27]] while the learning rate decays, so the specialised data is concentrated where the learning rate is low."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Training Time and Performance",
      "body_html": "The training run took about one hour on a single NVIDIA GPU at around [[4]][[5]] model FLOPs utilisation. There were no skipped steps. Validation loss was [[6]] nats per token on FineWeb-Edu and [[7]] on SQL code. Every source showed improvement at every checkpoint."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "How WikiSQL examples become training data",
      "body_html": "Each WikiSQL example turns into a pair: a prompt with the table schema as a [[1]] statement and the question, and a completion with the SQL. The loss is calculated from the completion alone."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Fine-Tuning Details",
      "body_html": "Fine-tuning uses a small batch size, a few warmup steps, cosine learning rate decay, no weight decay and a fixed number of epochs. Each epoch runs about as many steps as the number given, which takes around the time stated on the machine type listed. After each epoch, the model's accuracy on the first set of development questions is checked and the best epoch is saved."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Why Two Levels in WikiSQL",
      "body_html": "Two facts about WikiSQL motivate its two levels. About half of the model's invalid greedy outputs mention a column that does not exist. About a third of the gold condition values in the dev set appear verbatim in the question, ignoring case. So we define two levels. The first level is a byte-level grammar for the WikiSQL query shape that only allows the table's real column names. The second level also requires every condition value to be a substring of the question, Unicode-aware and valid. At each step, candidate tokens are checked best-first and the first that keeps the output a valid prefix is taken. This is equivalent to masking every invalid token and taking the argmax. The same decoder runs on the baselines through a byte-level adapter for their tokenizers."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Our Fine-Tuning Recipe",
      "body_html": "We fine-tuned models from [[1]] [[2]]~[[3]], [[4]]~[[5]], [[6]] and [[7]]~[[8]] using the same prompt text, loss masking, batch size, AdamW optimiser, schedule, epochs and epoch selection. The baselines accumulate gradients over token-weighted micro-batches, which a test shows gives the same gradient as the full batch to [[9]]. Our architecture was also fine-tuned from random weights with the same recipe."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Model Selection and Dev Questions",
      "body_html": "The models were picked based on the first set of development questions. The control was [[3]]. Figure~[[5]] shows the setup. Table~[[6]] in the appendix lists the models."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Single Test Score Per System",
      "body_html": "Every system was fine-tuned once with its development-chosen configuration and scored on the test set exactly once. No decision was made based on a test score."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Execution Accuracy Metric",
      "body_html": "Our main measure is how accurately the predicted queries match the gold queries when both are run against the table in a sandboxed read-only SQLite connection. We also use Salesforce's official WikiSQL evaluator, which gives scores for both execution and logical-form accuracy. When we feed the gold queries as predictions, it scores exactly [[1]][[2]] on the dev and test sets. Its execution accuracy matches ours within [[3]] points across all runs of the four main systems."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Accuracy Reporting",
      "body_html": "For each system, we report the average and standard deviation of per-question accuracy across multiple random seeds. We also give a percentile-bootstrap confidence interval over those questions, based on 1,000 resamples of the seed-averaged accuracies. "
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "headline": "System Comparison Methodology",
      "body_html": "When comparing two systems, we look at their predictions question by question. We use a paired bootstrap and the McNemar test on the questions where exactly one system gets it right. This lets us see which system performs better on those specific questions. "
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Fine-Tuning from Random Weights",
      "body_html": "A network fine-tuned from random weights reaches about the same performance as one pretrained on a large dataset. Pretraining on 1.3 trillion tokens adds about five percentage points to accuracy. Constrained decoding cannot replace that pretrained start."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Lead and Points on Two Metrics",
      "body_html": "One model is ahead by [[3]] points at greedy ([[4]]) and by [[5]] points with both constraints. On the development set, the greedy gap was only [[6]] points and not significant ([[7]]). So we report the test result: [[8]] is slightly better."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Pythia's Column Name Generation Edge",
      "body_html": "Pythia generates column names better than the model pointed to by [[1]]. It fails on [[3]] percent of test questions with invalid SQL, while our model fails on [[7]] percent. Our ablation study in Section~[[9]] shows the SQL-heavy training data explains only a small part of the gap, so we do not claim a clear cause."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Lead in Pretraining Token Count",
      "body_html": "The model with about [[3]][[4]] pretraining tokens is [[5]] points ahead on average across multiple seeds. The other model is [[7]] points behind."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Small Seed Standard Deviations",
      "body_html": "Seed standard deviations are about three points, which is much smaller than any gap between models. Our dev-to-test drop is about four points, close to the baselines' five points. Pythia, which has unstable fine-tuning, drops about six points."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Value Constraint Beats Schema Constraint",
      "body_html": "The value constraint matters more than the schema constraint for every model. Small models lose ground when they copy filter values exactly. For our model, constrained decoding turned a correct answer into a wrong one in every paired comparison we ran. It failed on both test sets and on the development set. For every baseline, it sometimes got it right, though plausibly because their tokenizers can force token splits they rarely produce. We did not verify this."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Small Proxy Model Tests One Design Choice at a Time",
      "body_html": "Table~[[1]] and Figure~[[2]] show how one design choice affects a small proxy model at a time. Rotary embeddings make the biggest difference: without them, accuracy falls by about [[3]] points and pretraining loss rises sharply. Tying embeddings improves accuracy by about [[6]] points, even though the untied model has more parameters. Grouped-query attention adds no measurable cost but shrinks the KV cache. Removing all SQL, code and Q&A data from pretraining costs only about [[12]] points after fine-tuning, despite the web-only model's higher loss on SQL code. Fine-tuning on [[15]] WikiSQL examples seems to recover much of what the SQL data taught."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "How Wrong Predictions Are Scored",
      "body_html": "Each wrong prediction is broken down into its parts: which column was chosen, whether it was grouped, and what conditions were added. These parts are then checked against the correct answer, which gives one category of error. Our model loses most on condition columns, values, and multi-part mistakes. These are the skills that need reading the table and the question. Aggregation errors are different: they stay at about 20% to 30% of test questions for every pretrained model, even though overall accuracy can vary by 4 percentage points."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Accuracy Drops With More Conditions",
      "body_html": "Accuracy falls when the gold query needs more conditions (Figure~[[1]]). On the [[2]] single-condition test questions, our model and [[3]] are level ([[4]][[5]] and [[6]][[7]], seed [[8]]). The gap widens with two conditions ([[9]][[10]] versus [[11]][[12]] on [[13]] questions), which matches the multi-part errors in Figure~[[14]]. Pythia drops fastest, from [[15]][[16]] to [[17]][[18]] at four conditions."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Why Our Model Failed on Aggregation",
      "body_html": "We checked [[1]] random dev questions where our model was wrong. In [[2]] of those, the gold aggregation did not match the question. For example, [[3]] is labelled [[4]] instead of the crowd figure, and [[5]] is labelled [[6]]. Another [[7]] were unclear, while only [[8]] were clear model errors. This suggests about [[9]] points of dev accuracy are lost to aggregation labels for every model, which matches earlier reports of annotation errors in WikiSQL."
    },
    {
      "index": 1,
      "headline": "Single Annotator Review",
      "body_html": "The audit had one annotator, the author, who did not blind the judgement. All [[10]] are available for re-checking."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "WikiSQL Query Limitations",
      "body_html": "WikiSQL queries cover only single-table operations with no joins, grouping or nesting. We do not test on harder benchmarks like Spider."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Baselines Differ in Pretraining Data and Architecture",
      "body_html": "The baselines differ in pretraining data, tokenizers and architecture, not just in token count. So Figure~[[2]] shows association, not a scaling law."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Validation on Dev Questions",
      "body_html": "Learning rates and epochs are chosen based on dev questions. The CI for these questions covers about 5 to 6 points, which is wider than the gap between any two learning rates."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Fine-Tuning Differences Between Runs",
      "body_html": "Fine-tuning a model on a GPU does not produce bit-for-bit identical results even when you set the random seed. For example, Pythia's test run after re-fine-tuning is different from its dev run."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "A Single Developer Can Build a Text-to-SQL Model",
      "body_html": "A single developer can build every part of a text-to-SQL language model, train it on about 1 million tokens in two days on one GPU, and get within about one point of the leader on WikiSQL while using less data than a model trained on [[3]][[4]]. Constrained decoding is a cheap and safe way to improve results at this scale. The last points still need much more training time, and on WikiSQL some errors come from the labels rather than the model."
    }
  ]
}

{
  "items": [
    {
      "index": 0,
      "headline": "Where to Find the Research Details",
      "body_html": "The code, configurations, prediction-level results, statistics and figures are in [[1]]. Every run, every number and every bug found along the way are recorded in [[2]]. Pretraining took about [[3]] [[4]] GPU-hours, and the benchmark (fine-tuning, evaluation and ablations) took about [[5]], both on NVIDIA [[6]] GPUs."
    }
  ]
}
